-- #1958 / #2032 — THE DISPATCHER-TRAP CONTROL, i.e. the shape the local pin used to
-- over-reject.
--
-- `main` has NO signature, so there is no enclosing rigid scope: `f`'s parameter var
-- cannot collapse anything, because there is nothing declared for it to collapse.
-- Every use is at a distinct GROUND type, which is exactly what the emitter's arg-tag
-- dispatch chain exists to discriminate. Rejecting this was the over-reject #2032
-- narrows away; `enclosingRigidScopeInPlay` is False here, so only the DICT channel
-- is consulted at the four genRestricted sites and `f` generalizes.
--
-- The sibling accept_1052_pinned_local_single_type and the reject_* pins guard the
-- other side of the boundary: with a declared `Sized a => …` signature in play, the
-- group's constraint monos ARE published, the gate opens, and the multi-type use is
-- still rejected with a located T-LOCAL-CONSTRAINED-MONO.
--
-- Ground truth, derived from the source, NOT captured: `speak` has a DEFAULT ("woof");
-- `Dog`'s impl overrides it with "woof", `Cat`'s with "meow", `Bell`'s with "ring".
-- Each `f` call must reach its own receiver's impl, so the three values in source
-- order are woof, meow, ring. A single collapsed route would print one of them three
-- times.
--
-- Verdict: ACCEPT.
interface Speak a where
  speak : a -> String
  speak _ = "woof"

data Dog = Dog

data Cat = Cat

data Bell = Bell

impl Speak Dog where
  speak _ = "woof"

impl Speak Cat where
  speak _ = "meow"

impl Speak Bell where
  speak _ = "ring"

main =
  let f = v => speak v
  println (f Dog ++ "|" ++ f Cat ++ "|" ++ f Bell)
